      ⍝ Bugs28 #50 regression: the result of V←λ (a named-lambda
      ⍝ assignment) used to be a fabricated character-vector VALUE
      ⍝ holding V's own name, which then leaked into any further
      ⍝ reduction as ordinary data -- e.g. (f←{⍵}) 5 became the 2-item
      ⍝ STRAND 'f' 5 instead of applying the lambda to 5. Fixed in
      ⍝ Prefix.cc's reduce_V_ASS_F_(): if an argument is already
      ⍝ waiting on the stack (right-to-left parsing: ssize() >
      ⍝ prefix_len detects it), the result is the function itself, so
      ⍝ a further reduction sees exactly what "F B" would see for any
      ⍝ other named function; otherwise (a bare V←λ statement, by far
      ⍝ the most common case, e.g. defining a helper lambda for later
      ⍝ use) the traditional printable/silent-assignment value result
      ⍝ is kept, so it behaves exactly like an ordinary value
      ⍝ assignment (no "missing mandatory right argument" error).
      ⍝
      (ZZ50F1←{⍵}) 5
5

      ⍝ control: with no argument waiting, a further reduction still
      ⍝ correctly rejects the leaked value (any error is acceptable;
      ⍝ this happens to be a SYNTAX ERROR since arithmetic requires a
      ⍝ numeric operand, not the function's committed name value)
      ⍝
      1+(ZZ50F2←{⍵})
SYNTAX ERROR+
      1+(ZZ50F2←{⍵})
      ^ ^

      )SIC

      ⍝ control: ⎕← of a bare V←λ statement still prints V's name,
      ⍝ exactly as before
      ⍝
      ⎕←ZZ50F3←{⍵}
ZZ50F3

      ⍝ the single most common real-world pattern: a bare V←λ
      ⍝ statement (nothing follows), used only later -- must NOT error
      ⍝
      ZZ50UT←{ (⍳⍺) ∘.> (⍳⍵)}
      ⎕NC 'ZZ50UT'
3

      2 ZZ50UT 2
0 0
1 0

      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #100(aa)): reduce_V_ASS_F_() (the same "named
      ⍝ lambda: V←{...}" phrase the #50 fix above touches) accepted ⎕
      ⍝ and the other distinguished symbols ⍺ ⍶ χ ⍹ ⍵ as V without
      ⍝ checking they were ordinary, user-assignable names -- unlike ⍞
      ⍝ and ⎕IO (which carry their own dedicated token tags and never
      ⍝ reach this generic phrase at all), ⎕/⍺/⍶/χ/⍹/⍵ carry the same
      ⍝ generic TC_SYMBOL class an ordinary name does, so ⎕←{⍵} silently
      ⍝ gave the lambda literal "⎕" as its new name instead of running
      ⍝ Quad_Quad::assign() (the code that implements ⎕←B) -- a no-op
      ⍝ with neither the SYNTAX ERROR nor the display every other
      ⍝ ⎕←function form (⎕←+/, ⎕←F) already correctly gives.
      ⍝
      ⎕←{⍵}
SYNTAX ERROR
      ⎕←{⍵}
      ^ ^

      )SIC

      ⍵←{⍺}
SYNTAX ERROR
      ⍵←{⍺}
      ^ ^

      )SIC

      ⍺←{⍵}
SYNTAX ERROR
      ⍺←{⍵}
      ^ ^

      )SIC

      χ←{⍵}
SYNTAX ERROR
      χ←{⍵}
      ^ ^

      )SIC

      ⍝ control: ⎕←F for an ordinary (non-lambda) function was already
      ⍝ correctly rejected, and is unaffected by this fix
      ⍝
      )ERASE ZZ100AA
      ⎕FX 'Z←ZZ100AA B' 'Z←B'
ZZ100AA

      ⎕←ZZ100AA
SYNTAX ERROR
      ⎕←ZZ100AA
      ^ ^

      )SIC
      )ERASE ZZ100AA

      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #94, Bugs27 #49 residual): a lambda rejected
      ⍝ at fix time by compute_lambda_body() (e.g. {⍶}1 -- a niladic
      ⍝ lambda referencing an operand) used to throw the raw DEFN_ERROR
      ⍝ macro, which calls update_error_info() -- at this point (before
      ⍝ the lambda's own statement has an SI entry) that rebuilds line
      ⍝ 2/3 from whatever the *previous* statement's frame was, and
      ⍝ also prints immediately, so the error was shown twice: once
      ⍝ (wrongly) under the previous statement, and once more from
      ⍝ Command::do_APL_expression()'s own catch block. Thrown directly
      ⍝ now, without calling update_error_info(): printed once, and
      ⍝ ⎕ET/⎕EM no longer leak the previous statement's info (⎕EM's
      ⍝ line 2/3 are simply empty, the same as on a fresh interpreter
      ⍝ with no previous statement to leak from).
      ⍝
      1÷0
DOMAIN ERROR
      1÷0
      ^^

      {⍶}1
DEFN ERROR+

      )MORE
niladic lambda with axis. left argument, or function argument(s)

      ⎕ET
0 2817

      ⎕EM
DEFN ERROR+
           
           

      )ERASE ZZ50F1 ZZ50F2 ZZ50F3 ZZ50UT
